{"agent_costs": {"main": 0.05087, "model-router:architect": 0.041777}, "answer": "The design is done, but `DESIGN.json` was not written. The subagent is read-only and its Bash attempt was denied, so nothing exists at the repository root yet. I haven't written it myself, since you asked me to hand the task off.\n\nThis is the subagent's proposed content:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"strategy\": \"event_invalidation\",\n  \"ttl_seconds\": null,\n  \"max_staleness_seconds\": 1,\n  \"reasons\": [\n    \"Each of the 12 servers subscribes to price_changed and evicts that product from its in-process cache; the bus delivers within 1s, so a changed price is served stale for at most 1s, under the 5s limit.\",\n    \"no_cache is impossible: 50,000 reads/s against a DB limit of 2,000 reads/s.\",\n    \"A TTL of 5s or less cannot meet the DB limit: with 100,000 products spread over 12 servers, most reads would miss and go to the DB, far above 2,000 reads/s.\",\n    \"A TTL long enough to keep DB load low would break the 5s staleness limit, so a TTL cannot be the primary mechanism.\",\n    \"write_through_from_admin is impossible: the admin service cannot reach the in-process caches directly.\",\n    \"Prices change only about 20 times a day and only via the admin service, which already publishes the event, so invalidation traffic is negligible and the cache hit rate stays near 100%.\",\n    \"The spec treats the 1s bus delivery as a guarantee, so no safety-net TTL is added and max staleness is the 1s bus latency.\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe reasoning is that each catalog server evicts a product when it receives `price_changed`. That keeps the hit rate high enough for the 2,000 reads/s DB limit and bounds staleness to the bus's 1s delivery, under the 5s limit.\n\nI can write this file myself or send it to an agent that has write access. Which do you want?", "api_error_status": null, "bench_commit": "335ef493fcb5f8e57c6318add60dd971447c21be", "bench_dirty": false, "budget_usd": 0.55, "class": "de